>
  <p>
    Most community onboarding experiences are designed around system events rather than human psychology. 
    A user enters a Discord server or community platform, triggers a bot event, receives an automated DM 
    with 14 bullet points of channel rules, and is promptly left alone in a sea of channels.
  </p>

  <p>
    This creates an immediate cognitive wall. The newcomer feels like an observer in a room where everyone 
    already knows the inside jokes, the unspoken norms, and the power hierarchies.
  </p>

  <h2>The 3 Horizons of Community Onboarding</h2>
  <p>
    Over a decade running community ecosystems across IAHGames, Wargaming, Google, and global crypto platforms, 
    we found that successful onboarding divides into three distinct emotional horizons:
  </p>

  <h3>1. Horizon Zero: Orienting (First 5 Minutes)</h3>
  <p>
    The goal is not to educate; it is to remove intimidation. The user needs to answer three instinctive questions:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Am I in the right place?</strong> (Clear mission statement and immediate social proof)</li>
    <li><strong>What is expected of me right now?</strong> (Single, low-friction micro-action)</li>
    <li><strong>Who is here to protect me?</strong> (Visible, benevolent moderation presence)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Horizon One: First Value Exchange (First 48 Hours)</h3>
  <p>
    True retention is born when a newcomer transitions from <em>consumer</em> to <em>contributor</em>. 
    This doesn't require a comprehensive essay; it can be as simple as answering a poll, receiving a welcome 
    reply from an existing trusted member (not a bot), or having their question solved in under 15 minutes.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Horizon Two: Social Embedding (First 30 Days)</h3>
  <p>
    A member stays in a community not for the brand, but for the relationships and status recognition they cultivate. 
    Creating pathways toward working groups, community champions, or specific interest cohorts cements long-term loyalty.
  </p>

  <h2>The Anti-Patterns to Avoid</h2>
  <blockquote>
    "Automating your welcome message is not onboarding. It is simply offloading the human effort of hospitality."
  </blockquote>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Channel Bloat:</strong> Presenting 40 channels upon first entry. Restrict access progressively.</li>
    <li><strong>Rule Interrogation:</strong> Requiring agreement to 20 legalistic conditions before viewing conversations.</li>
    <li><strong>Ghost Moderators:</strong> Where the only visible moderators are executing bans rather than facilitating dialogue.</li>
  </ul>
</ArticleLayout>
